WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.080
I grew up in the age of linear television,

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.960
when most people in Britain had a&nbsp;
choice of only four or five channels

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.880
and when a lot of the nation watched&nbsp;
the same shows at the same time.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.200
And when a medium has that much power,

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.040
it gets regulated heavily.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.720
Some of those regulations are sensible,

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.640
and some do sound a bit strange

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:17.920
if you've never heard of them before.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.480
I'm not including things that break other laws,

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.520
or anything vague like "cause offense"&nbsp;
that's true basically everywhere,

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.560
just with a different definition&nbsp;
of what "offense" means.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.120
These are things that you can publish on YouTube,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.080
that you might well be able to broadcast&nbsp;
in other countries in the world,

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.120
but that you cannot do on British television.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.920
Any demonstrations of hypnosis on stage&nbsp;
must be licensed: and that's still true.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.760
The fine for unlicensed&nbsp;
stage hypnosis can be £1,000.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.800
Local councils still regulate that.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.800
Because back in the 50s, that was a concern.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.240
Hypnosis was a bit scary.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.920
To the point where there was a glut of low&nbsp;
budget horror movies about hypnotic possession.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.640
So stage hypnosis was licensed,&nbsp;
and television hypnosis was banned.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.840
Now these days, that regulation isn't&nbsp;
there because they're worried about

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.440
some evil hypnotist taking over&nbsp;
the world through the television,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.160
although that was a plot point in terrifying&nbsp;
90s kids show The Demon Headmaster.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.840
Although, let's just say that child&nbsp;
actors have got a bit better direction

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:18.720
in the years since then.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.320
"I don't get it. I really Do Not Get It."

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.080
But better safe than sorry.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.320
If you did broadcast a hypnotic&nbsp;
induction to millions of people,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.360
then the chances are that&nbsp;
someone is going to believe

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.640
that it's done them some damage.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.040
Except. You can absolutely get&nbsp;
away with that if you're careful.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.360
In 2009 illusionist Derren Brown performed a stunt

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.400
in which he played out a "subliminal film"

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.800
which he claimed would make&nbsp;
some of the folks watching

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.920
feel like they were stuck to&nbsp;
their chair. He also said:

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.160
"The film is not effective at a lower resolution,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.640
"so if it gets posted on&nbsp;
the internet it won't work."

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:53.760
Which is ludicrous.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.960
British television was low-bitrate&nbsp;
standard-definition digital by then anyway.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.080
Those are lies.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.200
But they are a magician's lies,&nbsp;
so they're part of the show.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.960
"...find yourself stuck, locked, glued,&nbsp;
gelled, cemented, bonded, fused..."

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.360
That's a textbook hypnotic induction technique.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.440
I mean that literally, I&nbsp;
checked a hypnosis textbook.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.960
And there's almost two minutes of it,

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.360
complete with slow-eye contact zoom!

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.600
But he didn't say it was hypnosis.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.200
He never claimed to be hypnotizing people.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.400
He's just influencing with&nbsp;
some words and some wavy lines

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.800
and there were only five complaints&nbsp;
about it to the regulator,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.560
who didn't investigate.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.680
But officially and legally you can't&nbsp;
try to hypnotize people over the air.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.520
There's a list of sporting&nbsp;
events, defined by Parliament,

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.240
that must be available on free-to-air television.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.600
This goes back to 1996,

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.000
when paid television&nbsp;
channels, satellite and cable,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.240
were becoming a thing in Britain.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.800
Those services had a lot of money, and in theory,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.280
they could outbid the free-to-air&nbsp;
channels for things like

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.880
the Olympics, and Wimbledon, and&nbsp;
football and rugby cup finals,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.120
and, uh, some horse racing.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.880
So Parliament put a law in place:

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.240
pay-for television services&nbsp;
cannot broadcast any of those

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.240
unless the rights are also available at&nbsp;
a fair price to free-to-air channels.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.440
Although it also works the other way.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.000
Free-to-air channels can't lock&nbsp;
up exclusive rights either,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.920
because that would be unfair to the paid...

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.360
look, it's complicated and it's messy,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.640
and there are all sorts of rules to try and&nbsp;
establish some sort of level playing field

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.840
while also making sure that the World&nbsp;
Cup final doesn't go pay-per-view.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.680
Incidentally while I was&nbsp;
researching this I found that

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.160
other European countries&nbsp;
have a similar list of events

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.440
and there are some wonderful&nbsp;
national stereotypes in there.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.760
Austria: World Nordic Skiing Championships.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.680
Finland: the Ice Hockey World Championships.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.000
And Ireland: the hurling finals.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.040
This all makes sense.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.480
Governments do not want events&nbsp;
of national cultural importance,

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.160
and those sporting events are that,

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.920
they don't want those events to become irrelevant

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.320
because they're only available&nbsp;
to people who can pay.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.160
That's not in the modern sense of fake news,

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.760
although the regulations say reporting&nbsp;
does have to be neutral over here as well,

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.520
but fake news in the War of the&nbsp;
Worlds radio broadcast sense.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.760
You can't risk someone flipping through channels

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.440
only to land on your fictional news story

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.280
and think there's been some disaster.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.440
You can see this in action in Crisis Command,

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.560
a brilliant and ahead-of-its-time show from 2004

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.360
where three people are asked&nbsp;
to deal with a fictional crisis

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.120
as if they were government ministers.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.760
The story plays out through&nbsp;
fictional news broadcasts

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.120
that use actual BBC reporters&nbsp;
and actual BBC newsrooms.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:18.960
"Britain is under attack."

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.800
So to get around the rule, the&nbsp;
footage is always shown in context.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:23.760
Never full screen.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.120
There's always someone else in frame

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:26.800
or walking through the shot sometimes.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.320
And the same happens in drama shows.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.440
A news report about the end of the&nbsp;
world must always be in context.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.000
Now as far as I can tell no one's&nbsp;
trying to get around this lately.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.200
The last time I know of,

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.680
or at least the last time that&nbsp;
something sort-of like this happened,

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.520
was the now-legendary Ghostwatch,

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.120
on Halloween night, 1992.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.280
It pretended to be a live ghost investigation

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.760
hosted by genuine real-life trusted presenters

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.760
and of course: it Goes Wrong.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.680
Now, it was introduced in context:

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.120
"Screen One presents an unusual&nbsp;
and sometimes disturbing film..."

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.600
- but if you missed that context,&nbsp;
well, it was a clever show.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.760
It knew how to use and misuse the medium,

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.520
and it scared quite a lot of people.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.240
And it influenced a generation&nbsp;
of writers and producers.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.400
Not technically news, though.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.120
Given the tabloid fury afterwards,&nbsp;
no one's made anything similar since,

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.520
although after nearly 30 years we are&nbsp;
probably about due for someone to try it.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.480
There are exemptions for channels with

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.080
low budgets and small audience shares

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.760
and for broadcasters who are just starting out,

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.640
but even from day one, as an absolute minimum,

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.240
10% of a channel's output has&nbsp;
to have subtitles as an option.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.480
Once the channel's established&nbsp;
that goes up to 80%.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.880
Plus, 10% has to have optional audio description.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.400
5% must have sign language, even if&nbsp;
that's just added on a late night repeat.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.080
Accessibility should be a right and a legal duty

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.120
and occasionally channels do get&nbsp;
called out for forgetting that.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.200
Controversial opinion: this should&nbsp;
be true for YouTube as well.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.640
Not for small channels just starting out,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.840
but I do think that if you have&nbsp;
the sort of YouTube channel

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.560
that spends, let's say a decent amount of money,

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.160
or a decent amount of time on--

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.529
[Music]

00:05:57.842 --> 00:05:59.680
-- it's like two dollars a minute maximum,

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.092
or just a little bit of your time --

00:06:02.092 --> 00:06:04.892
[Music]

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.360
"Oh look at me, I've bought a Lamborghini!"

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.200
Buy some damn subtitles. Also...

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:17.520
I could do a whole video on&nbsp;
advertising law and product placement.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.160
I still intend to at some point,&nbsp;
I've been promising it for ages.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.760
But I do want to highlight just one specific rule:

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.840
12 minutes per hour.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:29.360
The maximum amount of commercials&nbsp;
allowed on all British channels.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.280
For some of the older channels it's even less,

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:34.160
seven or eight minutes per hour. And that's it.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:35.520
There are a few other rules about

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.746
how far apart commercial&nbsp;breaks
can be from each other 

00:06:37.746 --> 00:06:39.200
and how long they can be

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.400
and occasional small loopholes&nbsp;
for odd circumstances,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.080
but the big number is: 12&nbsp;
minutes per hour maximum.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.160
And that's not some rolling-average total.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.280
That's per hour on the clock,&nbsp;
which means that channels

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:54.400
will juggle their schedule by a&nbsp;
couple of minutes to fit things in.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.320
If there's a less popular show right&nbsp;
before a primetime ratings-grabber,

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.840
that less popular show might have&nbsp;
no adverts in the middle at all.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:03.200
Sure, they might still take a break,

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.280
show a couple of trailers for their own channel

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.400
that don't count towards&nbsp;
that big time on the clock,

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.720
but the schedulers will make sure that

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.960
there's as much advertising as possible

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.000
just before the top of the&nbsp;
hour, just before the big show,

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.000
and then just after the top of the next hour.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.000
There is an art to fitting all that in,

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.280
and out of all these rules,

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:23.680
this is the one thing that&nbsp;
you cannot get away with.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:25.360
The regulator checks. They will notice

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:26.880
and many channels have been pulled up on it.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.360
But there was one thing that I did&nbsp;
notice while I was researching this.

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:33.680
There is one other line in&nbsp;
those rules which says that

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:35.920
commercial breaks must be taken in a way

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.560
that doesn't disrupt the flow of the program.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.120
I explicitly rules out that
Who&nbsp;Wants To Be A Millionaire trick

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.960
where someone has just chosen their answer,

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.880
and it's all dramatic, and the host says...

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:46.720
"Time for a break."

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.360
Like, it's really clear that's not allowed.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:51.360
There's no other way to read that sentence.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:54.560
But the show's hosts have been&nbsp;
doing that for 20 years now

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:56.240
and they've never been called on it.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:11.840
There are some rules, apparently,&nbsp;
where entertainment can come first.

